# XBD 3 Definitions
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The chapter defines terms that the rest of the standard uses; almost every
# definition is exercised where it is used (in the function, header and
# utility modules). The assertions here are the definitions that say
# something a program can check by itself: the blank and white-space
# characters of the POSIX locale, the characters a portable file name may
# hold, the size and content of a file name, and the range of a file
# descriptor. The 3.254 pathname rule on repeated slashes is tested with
# XBD 4.16 (pathname resolution).

assertion  XBD.3-definitions.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.45
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.346
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.383
class      required
since      1992
says       In the POSIX locale the blank characters are exactly the space and
           the tab: of all 256 byte values only those two are blank for
           isblank(), and iswblank() agrees for the same character codes.
test       t/001-blank-characters.c

assertion  XBD.3-definitions.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.413
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.415
class      required
since      1992
says       In the POSIX locale the white-space characters are exactly the
           space, tab, newline, carriage-return, form-feed and vertical-tab:
           of all 256 byte values only those six satisfy isspace(), and
           iswspace() agrees for the same character codes.
test       t/002-white-space-characters.c

assertion  XBD.3-definitions.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.265
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.264
class      required
since      1992
says       A file can be created, read back, listed and removed under each
           name made of the portable filename characters (the 26 uppercase
           and 26 lowercase letters, the ten digits, period, underscore and
           hyphen-minus): every one-character name except the dot itself,
           names with a leading, trailing or inner period, an underscore or
           a hyphen-minus, and a 14-byte name that uses all kinds of
           character.
note       Each name is created, read and removed in turn, so a file system
           that does not tell upper and lower case apart (a permitted
           extension) does not change the result.
test       t/003-portable-filename-characters.sh

assertion  XBD.3-definitions.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.146
class      required
since      1992
says       A file can be created with a name of exactly {NAME_MAX} bytes
           (pathconf(_PC_NAME_MAX) for the directory) and the directory then
           lists it under that name; no name read from a directory holds a
           slash, and a path with a slash in the middle names a file in a
           directory and never creates a file whose name holds the slash.
note       NOTINUSE when pathconf reports no limit or a limit above 1000.
test       t/004-filename-bytes.c

assertion  XBD.3-definitions.005
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.141
class      required
since      1992
says       A newly created file descriptor (from open(), dup() or pipe())
           is not negative and is below sysconf(_SC_OPEN_MAX).
note       NOTINUSE when sysconf reports no limit.
test       t/005-descriptor-range.c
